n `deadline_exceeded`. The spec Desired Behavior #2: \"A failed Job whose condition reason is `BackoffLimitExceeded` reports a distinct pod-failure reason — never `deadline_exceeded` — regardless of whether the pod is still resolvable.\" And Failure Mode row 1: \"BackoffLimitExceeded Job whose pod is already GC'd / not in the pod-informer cache | Still publishes a distinct pod-failure reason, never `deadline_exceeded`; the reason carries no pod detail if none is resolvable\".\n\nSo for BackoffLimitExceeded without resolvable pod → distinct pod-failure reason without detail = `pod_crash_no_stdout` (the existing generic pod-failure reason).\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider whether \"generic pod-failure reason\" = `ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout`. The spec Desired Behavior #4 says: \"Other non-zero terminated reasons fall back to the existing generic pod-failure reason\". The existing generic pod-failure reason in the codebase is `ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout` (the catch-all in classifyPodFailure for PodFailed + non-zero exit, and in JobFailureReason for \"any other Failed condition\"). Yes, that's the generic one.\n\nSo the design:\n\n`JobFailureReason(job)`:\n- DeadlineExceeded → `ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded`\n- BackoffLimitExceeded → `ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout` (distinct, generic pod-failure)\n- else → `ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout`\n\nWait, but that makes BackoffLimitExceeded return the same value as the \"else\" branch. Is that a problem? No — the point is it's distinct from deadline_exceeded. The pod-detail resolution happens at HandleJob time.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider. Maybe a cleaner design is to have a separate function that HandleJob uses to compose the final reason string with pod detail, e.g.:\n\n```go\nfunc (w *jobWatcher) resolveFailureReason(ctx, job, baseReason) string\n```\n\nHmm, but let me think about the flow more carefully. In `HandleJob`:\n\n```go\nif IsJobFailed(job) {\n    reason := JobFailureReason(job)\n    glog.V(2).Infof(\"jo