{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_35414411a4404fdb938ec2b3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Important findings:\n1. The spawner sets `BackoffLimit=0` (`jobBuilder.SetBackoffLimit(0)`). So a spawned Job fails after a single failed pod — but a Job with backoffLimit=0 still gets the `BackoffLimitExceeded` condition when its pod fails (the Job controller sets `BackoffLimitExceeded` on the JobFailed condition when the pod fails and backoff limit is 0 — actually with backoffLimit=0, the Job fails immediately with condition reason... let me think. With backoffLimit=0, when a pod fails, the Job controller marks the Job failed. The condition reason for a Job with backoffLimit=0... Hmm. Actually per k8s docs, when backoffLimit is 0 and a pod fails, the JobFailed condition reason is `BackoffLimitExceeded`. Yes — k8s sets reason \"BackoffLimitExceeded\" when the number of pod failures exceeds backoffLimit (including 0). So with backoffLimit=0, a single failed pod → JobFailed condition reason `BackoffLimitExceeded`.\n\nSo in practice there's typically ONE pod per Job. But the spec's failure mode row 4 mentions multiple pods, so handle by picking the last pod. Note `jobBuilder.SetBackoffLimit(0)` means `BackoffLimit` is set on the spec. This confirms the real-world path: pod fails → Job condition BackoffLimitExceeded.\n\nWait — this is interesting. With backoffLimit=0, the \"last pod\" selection is trivial (one pod). But the spec explicitly lists the multi-pod failure mode, so I'll keep the \"latest pod\" selection logic. It's cheap.\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider — with backoffLimit=0 there's only ever one pod (no retries). So \"multiple pods with different terminated reasons\" wouldn't happen for spawned jobs... but the spec lists it as a failure mode anyway. The spec says \"The terminal failure reflects the last pod's terminated state (the one that exhausted the backoff limit)\". With one pod this is trivial. I'll implement latest-by-CreationTimestamp selection to be safe, matching the spec's failure mode. It's a few lines.\n\nHmm, but actually — is it worth the complexity? Let me reconsider. The failure mode is listed, so per the rule \"every row in the spec's Failure Modes table must appear as a requirement step\". So I must address it. Picking the most recently created pod among owned pods handles it. Good.\n\n2. `fmt.Sprintf` IS used in result_publisher.go (lines 183, 275) — so `fmt.Sprintf` for string building is an accepted pattern. Good. I'll use it for composing the detail string.\n\n3. `handleTerminal` and `publishSyntheticFailure` have single callers within job_watcher.go — safe to change their signatures to `string`.\n\n4. CHANGELOG: `## v0.8.0` is the latest (HEAD). No `## Unreleased`. The spec says add `## Unreleased` section with a bullet for this fix. The changelog-guide says write `## Unreleased` immediately after implementing. Good.\n\nNow let me also verify the sweeper test's `makePod` helper to reuse the exact pod shape in the internal test file. Let me look at the sweeper test's makePod signature. From grep: `makePod(\"pod-failed\", \"test-ns\", string(taskID), corev1.PodFailed, 0)` — 5 args: name, namespace, taskID, phase, ??? (maybe exitCode). Let me check. Actually for the internal test file I'll write my own helpers anyway.\n\nLet me look at the sweeper test's makePod to match conventions. Also let me check `ResultPublisher` fake mock — `mocks.FakeResultPublisher` with `PublishFailureArgsForCall`. The external test uses that. For the internal test file, same mock.\n\nNow, let me also double check the `corev1listers` import path and `labels` import for the lister listing. From zombie_sweeper.go:\n```go\n\"k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/labels\"\ncorev1listers \"k8s.io/client-go/listers/core/v1\"\n```\nAnd lister.Pods(ns).List(selector). Good.\n\nNow — one thing to reconsider: in `terminalPodDetail`, I plan to list pods via `lister.Pods(job.Namespace).List(labels.Everything())`. But wait — there's a subtlety. The informer was created with `WithTweakListOptions(LabelSelector = \"agent.benjamin-borbe.de/task-id\")`. The lister's List() filters from the cache. Since the cache only contains pods with that label, `labels.Everything()` returns all cached pods (all task-id-labeled). Then filter by ownerJobName == job.Name. Good.\n\nAlternatively, I could list by the task-id label selector like the sweeper does. But ownerRef matching is what the spec prefers (\"matched by the Job owner reference or the task-id label\"). ownerJobName already exists. I'll use ownerRef matching.\n\nNow, about the \"latest pod\" selection: compare `pod.CreationTimestamp`. Actually — hmm, the \"pod that exhausted the backoff limit\" is the LAST pod created (each retry creates a new pod). CreationTimestamp is the right sort key. Let me use it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider: in a Job with backoffLimit > 0, the LAST pod is the one that exhausted the limit. The last pod has the latest CreationTimestamp. Good.\n\nNow — should `terminalPodDetail` require `ExitCode != 0`? Yes — a terminated container with exit code 0 is not a failure. The pod we care about has a failed terminated container. Let me require `cs.State.Terminated.ExitCode != 0`. Also handle reason empty (some terminations have empty Reason — e.g., exitCode 1 with no reason set). If Reason is empty, the composed string would be `pod_crash_no_stdout (/1)`. Hmm. The spec AC2(b) uses `Error`/exit `1` example. For robustness, when reason is empty, fall back to `\"Error\"`? Hmm — the spec says \"surfaces that pod's own terminated reason + exit code\". If reason is empty, surfacing `(/1)` is ugly. But let me not over-engineer — the spec leaves formatting to impl. I could map empty reason to \"Error\" (kubelet uses \"Error\" for generic non-zero exits). Actually, kubelet sets terminated reason \"Error\" for non-zero exits with no specific reason. And for OOMKilled it sets \"OOMKilled\". So in practice reason won't be empty for the cases we care about. I'll keep it simple: use the terminated reason as-is. If empty, `( /1)` — hmm. \n\nActually, let me reconsider — to keep it robust and match the AC2(b) example (`Error`/exit `1`), I'll specify: the composition uses the terminated reason verbatim. The test uses a pod with Reason: \"Error\", ExitCode: 1. So the prompt's tests pin the format with real data; empty-reason edge is not in scope.\n\nHmm, but actually — one more consideration on the composition: what about a terminated pod where Reason is \"OOMKilled\" but there are multiple containers? The first non-zero-terminated container's state. Fine.\n\nOK. Now let me also verify: the existing HandleJob test \"publishes synthetic failure and deletes job on Failed state\" (line 84) uses `failedCondition(\"OOMKilled\")` — a JobFailed condition with Message \"OOMKilled\" but NO Reason field! So `c.Reason` is \"\" → `JobFailureReason` returns `ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout` → publishes `pod_crash_no_stdout`. This test stays valid. Good.\n\nThe test \"uses pod_crash_no_stdout when condition has no message\" (line 200) — JobFailed condition, no Reason, no Message → `pod_crash_no_stdout`. Stays valid. Good.\n\nNow the two DeadlineExceeded tests (HandleJob path line 220, mapping path line 262) stay — they assert `deadline_exceeded`. Good.\n\nThe two BackoffLimitExceeded tests:\n- HandleJob path line 240: condition Reason BackoffLimitExceeded, no pod in lister (lister nil since Run not called) → HandleJob should publish... `failureReason(job)`: JobFailureReason → ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout; isBackoffLimitExceeded → true; backoffFailureReason → terminalPodDetail → lister nil → ok=false → return base.String() = \"pod_crash_no_stdout\". So the updated test asserts `pod_crash_no_stdout`. \n\nWait, but hold on — is that the right assertion? The spec AC1 says the updated Ginkgo assertion (both HandleJob path and direct mapping) asserts `BackoffLimitExceeded` no longer yields `deadline_exceeded`. It doesn't prescribe the exact new value for the no-pod HandleJob case. `pod_crash_no_stdout` (distinct from deadline_exceeded) satisfies it. \n\nHmm, but wait — should the HandleJob-path BackoffLimitExceeded test with no pod assert `pod_crash_no_stdout` exactly? That's what my design produces. Yes. The test would be renamed to \"maps BackoffLimitExceeded job condition to pod_crash_no_stdout (no resolvable pod)\".\n\n- mapping path line 271: `JobFailureReason` for BackoffLimitExceeded → `ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout`. Updated assertion.\n\nNow, new tests needed (per ACs):\n- AC1: direct mapping test (update line 271) + grep check. Done by the two updates.\n- AC2(a): OOMKilled pod resolvable → HandleJob publishes reason containing \"OOMKilled\", \"137\" (and \"pod_oom_killed\").\n- AC2(b): non-OOMKilled terminated pod (Error/1) → published reason contains \"Error\", \"1\", and neither \"deadline_exceeded\" nor \"pod_oom_killed\".\n- AC3: PublishFailure called with the specific reason string — covered by AC2(a) asserting ContainSubstring(\"pod_oom_killed\") OR a dedicated equal check. The spec AC3 says \"unit test asserts PublishFailure was called with the specific reason string (e.g. pod_oom_killed for an OOMKilled pod)\". I'll have the OOMKilled test assert the full composed string equals `pod_oom_killed (OOMKilled/137)` — that covers AC2(a) (contains OOMKilled, 137) AND AC3 (specific reason) in one assertion. Actually to be safe, I'll assert both: Equal(\"pod_oom_killed (OOMKilled/137)\") which implies contains. Good.\n- AC4: OOMKilled NOT reported as deadline_exceeded — the same OOMKilled test asserts the reason is not \"deadline_exceeded\" (Equal to the composed string covers it). But AC4 wants a \"new unit test with an OOMKilled pod asserts a distinct reason is published\". I can add an explicit `Not(Equal(\"deadline_exceeded\"))` assertion or a dedicated test. I'll fold it into the OOMKilled HandleJob test with an explicit `Expect(calledReason).NotTo(ContainSubstring(\"deadline_exceeded\"))` or just the Equal assertion. To be safe and match AC4's wording, I'll include an explicit assertion.\n\n- Failure mode \"pod lister not yet synced / nil\" → already covered by the updated HandleJob BackoffLimitExceeded test (no lister → pod_crash_no_stdout). Also maybe add a direct test of `terminalPodDetail` returning ok=false when lister nil. Hmm, terminalPodDetail is internal (internal test file). I can add an internal test for it.\n\n- Failure mode \"pod already GC'd / not in cache\" → same as nil lister → pod_crash_no_stdout without detail. Could add an internal test where lister is populated but has no matching pod → ok=false → publish \"pod_crash_no_stdout\" bare. I'll add this.\n\n- Failure mode \"Job exhausts backoff across multiple pods with different terminated reasons\" → internal test: two pods owned by the job, first Error/1, second OOMKilled/137 (latest creation) → publishes pod_oom_killed (OOMKilled/137). This is a good boundary test.\n\n- Failure mode \"condition flips BackoffLimitExceeded → DeadlineExceeded\": final reason reflects the last observed condition. My design: if the failed condition reason is DeadlineExceeded → deadline_exceeded; BackoffLimitExceeded → pod-failure. If both conditions present... `JobFailureReason` iterates conditions in order and returns on the first JobFailed=True condition. If a job has both BackoffLimitExceeded and DeadlineExceeded conditions... rare. The spec says \"either mapping is specific, never a silent merge\". My design: `JobFailureReason` returns based on the first matched condition. If DeadlineExceeded appears first → deadline_exceeded. Hmm, is that \"the terminal condition observed\"? The deadline one is the more terminal. This is an edge case. The spec's failure mode says \"Final reason reflects the terminal condition observed; either mapping is specific, never a silent merge\". My design satisfies \"specific, never silent merge\" as long as we never re