— not realistic. I'll accept it as defensive (glog + return false). The rest of the branches are covered.\n- no owned pod found → covered by test 4.\n- pod found but no non-zero terminated container → hmm, is that covered? A pod owned by the job but with no non-zero terminated container (e.g. running or waiting) → the inner loop skips it → best stays nil → ok=false. Not explicitly tested. Could add: pod owned by job but in Running phase → publish pod_crash_no_stdout bare. Let me add that as a variant or fold into test 4 by having setPodLister include a running pod. Actually let me add a small test: pod owned by job but not terminated → generic reason without detail. Hmm, is that a realistic scenario? BackoffLimitExceeded with a still-running pod — unusual (backoff limit is hit after pod failures). But defensive. I could add it. Let me add it as part of the coverage — actually, let me keep the test set tight but sufficient:\n\nTests in internal file:\n1. OOMKilled pod → `pod_oom_killed (OOMKilled/137)` (AC2a/AC3/AC4)\n2. Error/1 pod → `pod_crash_no_stdout (Error/1)` (AC2b)\n3. Multi-pod last OOMKilled → `pod_oom_killed (OOMKilled/137)` (DB row 4)\n4. Empty lister cache → `pod_crash_no_stdout` (DB row 1/3)\n\nThat's 4 internal tests. Plus the external updates (2 wrong tests → correct), plus zombie_reason_test.go new assertion.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider whether to also add the \"pod owned but not terminated\" case. It's an edge. The coverage rule: new code ≥80%. The `terminalPodDetail` function has these branches:\n- lister nil → return false (covered by external updated test — wait, that test goes through HandleJob which calls failureReason → backoffFailureReason → terminalPodDetail with nil lister. Yes covered).\n- List error → return false (NOT covered, defensive)\n- no matching owned pod → return false (covered by test 4)\n- matching pod but no non-zero terminated → best nil → return false (NOT covered unless I add it)\n- best pod found, terminated non-zero → return true (covered by tests 1,2,3)\n\nSo two uncovered branches (List error, no-terminated-container). Both are small defensive branches. To boost coverage, I could add one test where the owned pod is Running (not terminated). Let me add it — it's cheap and covers the \"no non-zero terminated container\" branch:\n\n5. **Owned pod not terminated (Running) → generic reason without detail**:\n```go\nIt(\"publishes the generic pod-failure reason when the owned pod has no non-zero terminated container\", func() {\n    job := makeJob(\"job-running\", string(testTaskID), failedBackoffCondition())\n    pod := makePod(\"pod-running\", \"job-running\", string(testTaskID), corev1.PodRunning, 0)\n    setPodLister(watcher, pod)\n    taskStore.Store(testTaskID, testTask)\n    watcher.HandleJob(ctx, job)\n    Expect(fakePublisher.PublishFailureCallCount()).To(Equal(1))\n    _, _, _, calledReason := fakePublisher.PublishFailureArgsForCall(0)\n    Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"pod_crash_no_stdout\"))\n})\n```\n\nThat covers the no-terminated branch. The List-error branch stays defensive (glog only). Good enough for ≥80%.\n\nNow let me write the makePod helper shapes:\n\n```go\nmakeFailedBackoffJob := func(name, taskID string) *batchv1.Job {\n    return &batchv1.Job{\n        ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{\n            Name:      name,\n            Namespace: \"test-ns\",\n            Labels:    map[string]string{\"agent.benjamin-borbe.de/task-id\": taskID},\n        },\n        Status: batchv1.JobStatus{\n            Conditions: []batchv1.JobCondition{{\n                Type:    batchv1.JobFailed,\n                Status:  corev1.ConditionTrue,\n                Reason:  \"BackoffLimitExceeded\",\n                Message: \"Job has reached the specified backoff limit\",\n            }},\n        },\n    }\n}\n\nmakeOwnedTerminatedPod := func(name, jobName, taskID, terminatedReason string, exitCode int32, createdAt string) *corev1.Pod {\n    pod := &corev1.Pod{\n        ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{\n            Name:            name,\n            Namespace:       \"test-ns\",\n            Labels:          map[string]string{\"agent.benjamin-borbe.de/task-id\": taskID},\n            OwnerReferences: []metav1.OwnerReference{{Kind: \"Job\", Name: jobName}},\n        },\n        Status: corev1.PodStatus{\n            Phase: corev1.PodFailed,\n            ContainerStatuses: []corev1.ContainerStatus{{\n                State: corev1.ContainerState{\n                    Terminated: &corev1.ContainerStateTerminated{\n                        Reason:   terminatedReason,\n                        ExitCode: exitCode,\n                    },\n                },\n            }},\n        },\n    }\n    if createdAt != \"\" {\n        t, _ := time.Parse(time.RFC3339, createdAt)\n        pod.CreationTimestamp = metav1.NewTime(t)\n    }\n    return pod\n}\n```\n\nHmm — wait, one important detail about the informer indexer. The sweeper test uses `informerFactory := k8sinformers.NewSharedInformerFactoryWithOptions(fakeClient, 0, k8sinformers.WithNamespace(\"test-ns\"))` and adds pods with namespace \"test-ns\" to the indexer. The `Pods().Lister().Pods(\"test-ns\")` retrieves by namespace. Good — my pods use namespace \"test-ns\".\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the shared informer's indexer for Pods uses namespace as part of the key. When I `GetIndexer().Add(pod)`, it indexes by namespace/name. The lister `Pods(\"test-ns\").List(Everything())` lists from the indexer for that namespace. Good.\n\nNow — a question about `terminalPodDetail` using `lister.Pods(job.Namespace).List(labels.Everything())`. The real lister (from Run) is namespace-restricted too (WithNamespace). And the test lister is namespace \"test-ns\", job namespace \"test-ns\". Match. Good.\n\nNow let me also reconsider: should `terminalPodDetail` filter by task-id label too, in addition to ownerRef? The spec says \"matched by the Job owner reference or the task-id label\". ownerRef alone is sufficient and precise. Since the informer cache is already filtered to task-id-labeled pods, ownerRef match is enough. Good.\n\nNow, the `failureReason` method and helpers — let me write them precisely:\n\n```go\n// failureReason returns the reason string to publish for a failed Job. A\n// BackoffLimitExceeded failure surfaces the pod's terminated reason + exit code\n// when the pod is still resolvable (see backoffFailureReason); every other\n// failure publishes the bare ZombieReason value.\nfunc (w *jobWatcher) failureReason(job *batchv1.Job) string {\n    reason := JobFailureReason(job)\n    if reason == ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded || !isBackoffLimitExceeded(job) {\n        return reason.String()\n    }\n    return w.backoffFailureReason(job, reason)\n}\n\n// backoffFailureReason classifies a BackoffLimitExceeded Job using its resolved\n// pod's terminal state: the dedicated pod_oom_killed for an OOMKilled pod, else\n// the generic pod-failure reason, always appending the terminated reason + exit\n// code when a pod is resolvable so operators see the cause without inspecting\n// the pod.\nfunc (w *jobWatcher) backoffFailureReason(job *batchv1.Job, base ZombieReason) string {\n    terminatedReason, exitCode, ok := w.terminalPodDetail(job)\n    if !ok {\n        return base.String()\n    }\n    if terminatedReason == \"OOMKilled\" {\n        return fmt.Sprintf(\"%s (%s/%d)\", ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled, terminatedReason, exitCode)\n    }\n    return fmt.Sprintf(\"%s (%s/%d)\", base.String(), terminatedReason, exitCode)\n}\n\n// terminalPodDetail returns the terminated reason and exit code of the Job's\n// most recently created pod that has a non-zero terminated container, matched by\n// Job owner reference. ok is false when the pod lister is not yet synced, the\n// lister lookup fails, or no owned pod carries a non-zero terminated container.\nfunc (w *jobWatcher) terminalPodDetail(job *batchv1.Job) (terminatedReason string, exitCode int32, ok bool) {\n    lister := w.PodLister()\n    if lister == nil {\n        return \"\", 0, false\n    }\n    pods, err := lister.Pods(job.Namespace).List(labels.Everything())\n    if err != nil {\n        glog.Errorf(\"job watcher: list pods for job %s/%s: %v\", job.Namespace, job.Name, err)\n        return \"\", 0, false\n    }\n    var best *corev1.Pod\n    for _, pod := range pods {\n        if ownerJobName(pod) != job.Name {\n            continue\n        }\n        if !hasNonZeroTerminatedContainer(pod) {\n            continue\n        }\n        if best == nil || pod.CreationTimestamp.After(best.CreationTimestamp.Time) {\n            best = pod\n        }\n    }\n    if best == nil {\n        return \"\", 0, false\n    }\n    for _, cs := range best.Status.ContainerStatuses {\n        if cs.State.Terminated != nil && cs.State.Terminated.ExitCode != 0 {\n            return cs.State.Terminated.Reason, cs.State.Terminated.ExitCode, true\n        }\n    }\n    return \"\", 0, false\n}\n\n// hasNonZeroTerminatedContainer reports whether any container has terminated\n// with a non-zero exit code.\nfunc hasNonZeroTerminatedContainer(pod *corev1.Pod) bool {\n    for _, cs := range pod.Status.ContainerStatuses {\n        if cs.State.Terminated != nil && cs.State.Terminated.ExitCode != 0 {\n            return true\n        }\n    }\n    return false\n}\n```\n\nWait — `fmt.Sprintf(\"%s (%s/%d)\", ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled, ...)` — `%s` on a ZombieReason calls String(). Fine. But mixing `%s` with a Stringer and an int — fine.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider whether the `hasNonZeroTerminatedContainer` helper is worth it or if I should inline. It's used twice (in the loop to skip, and in the final extraction). It's small and clear. Keep it.\n\nWait — actually there's redundancy: the `best` selection requires `hasNonZeroTerminatedContainer(pod)`, and then the final loop re-scans to extract. That's fine.\n\nNow `isBackoffLimitExceeded`:\n```go\n// isBackoffLimitExceeded reports whether the Job's terminal Failed condition has\n// reason \"BackoffLimitExceeded\" (the Job exhausted its retry backoff limit).\nfunc isBackoffLimitExceeded(job *batchv1.Job) bool {\n    for _, c := range job.Status.Conditions {\n        if c.Type == batchv1.JobFailed && c.Status == corev1.ConditionTrue && c.Reason == \"BackoffLimitExceeded\" {\n            return true\n        }\n    }\n    return false\n}\n```\n\nNow `JobFailureReason` new version:\n```go\n// JobFailureReason maps a failed Job's conditions to a ZombieReason. Returns\n// ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded when a Failed condition has Reason\n// \"DeadlineExceeded\" (the kubelet killed the pod for running past\n// activeDeadlineSeconds). Returns the generic pod-failure reason\n// ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout for \"BackoffLimitExceeded\" (the Job exhausted\n// its retry backoff limit — a distinct reason from a deadline kill; HandleJob\n// resolves the pod's terminated reason + exit code and surfaces them in the\n// published reason) and for any other Failed condition (the pod terminated\n// non-zero and no AgentResult was observed; the Job-condition informer only\n// fires AFTER terminal state, so absence of an AgentResult is implicit at this\n// point).\nfunc JobFailureReason(job *batchv1.Job) ZombieReason {\n    for _, c := range job.Status.Conditions {\n        if c.Type == batchv1.JobFailed && c.Status == corev1.ConditionTrue {\n            switch c.Reason {\n            case \"DeadlineExceeded\":\n                return ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded\n            case \"BackoffLimitExceeded\":\n                return ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    return ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout\n}\n```\n\nNow `zombie_reason.go` addition:\n```go\nconst (\n    ZombieReasonImagePullBackOff  ZombieReason = \"image_pull_backoff\"\n    ZombieReasonPodEvicted        ZombieReason = \"pod_evicted\"\n    ZombieReasonDeadlineExceeded  ZombieReason = \"deadline_exceeded\"\n    ZombieReasonPodNotScheduled   ZombieReason = \"pod_not_scheduled\"\n    ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout  ZombieReason = \"pod_crash_no_stdout\"\n    ZombieReasonExecutorWatchLost ZombieReason = \"executor_watch_lost\"\n    ZombieReasonTypeMismatch      ZombieReason = \"type_mismatch\"\n    ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled      ZombieReason = \"pod_oom_killed\"\n)\n```\n\nAnd doc comment update.\n\nNow — the external test updates:\n1. Line 240: rename + assert `pod_crash_no_stdout`:\n```go\nIt(\"maps BackoffLimitExceeded job condition to pod_crash_no_stdout (no resolvable pod)\", func() {\n    ...\n    Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"pod_crash_no_stdout\"))\n})\n```\nWait — with the external watcher (no lister), HandleJob → failureReason → backoff → terminalPodDetail → lister nil → \"pod_crash_no_stdout\". Good.\n\nHmm — but hold on. Is `pod_crash_no_stdout` the best assertion, or should it be more descriptive? The spec AC1 says \"BackoffLimitExceeded no longer yields deadline_exceeded\". The value `pod_crash_no_stdout` satisfies this. But let me reconsider whether the test should assert something that proves it's NOT deadline_exceeded explicitly. I'll do both: `Expect(calledReason).To(Equal(\"pod_crash_no_stdout\"))` and maybe `Expect(calledReason).ToNot(Equal(\"deadline_exceeded\"))`. The Equal assertion already proves not-deadline. I'll keep just Equal plus the doc rename. Actually, to be robust and match AC wording (\"asserts BackoffLimitExceeded no longer yields deadline_exceeded\"), I'll keep `Equal(\"pod_crash_no_stdout\")` — that's unambiguous.\n\n2. Line 271: `JobFailureReason` direct mapping:\n```go\nIt(\"returns pod_crash_no_stdout for BackoffLimitExceeded\", func() {\n    ...\n    Expect(pkg.JobFailureReason(job)).To(Equal(pkg.ZombieReasonPodCrashNoStdout))\n})\n```\n\n3. zombie_reason_test.go: add:\n```go\nIt(\"returns pod_oom_killed for ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled\", func() {\n    Expect(pkg.ZombieReasonPodOOMKilled.String()).To(Equal(\"pod_oom_killed\"))\n})\n```\n\nNow — the CHANGELOG. Add at top (after `# Changelog