\nBut wait — there's a subtle issue with internal test files and the Ginkgo test suite structure. The repo probably has a suite test runner file. Let me check how the Ginkgo suite is bootstrapped. If `job_watcher_internal_test.go` is in `package pkg` and defines `var _ = Describe(...)`, it needs to be part of the Ginkgo suite that's run. Let me check for a suite file (e.g., `pkg_suite_test.go`). The internal test file in `package pkg` would need to use the same suite runner. Let me check.\n\nActually, the repo has `main_internal_test.go` in `package main` — and there must be a `main_test.go` with `RunSpecs` in `package main_test`. Wait, the two files are in different packages (`main` and `main_test`) but they're in the same directory. In Go, you can't have two different package names in the same directory unless one is a `_test` external package. `package main` (in main_internal_test.go) and `package main_test` (in main_test.go) — this is the standard internal/external test pattern: internal test files use `package main`, external use `package main_test`. Both compile into the test binary. For Ginkgo, the suite is bootstrapped in one place (main_test.go calls RunSpecs). The internal file's `var _ = Describe(...)` registers specs into the suite. ✓\n\nSo for `pkg/job_watcher_internal_test.go` in `package pkg`, there must be a suite bootstrap in the pkg tests. Let me check where RunSpecs is called for the pkg package. There might be a `pkg_suite_test.go` or similar. Let me search.\n\nActually — this is important. If the new internal test file `job_watcher_internal_test.go` is in `package pkg` and there's already an external test file `job_watcher_test.go` in `package pkg_test`, then both packages need suite bootstrap. The Ginkgo suite for `package pkg` would need a `RunSpecs` call somewhere in `package pkg` test files. Let me check whether the repo has a `package pkg` test that runs specs, or whether it's a single external `pkg_test` suite.\n\nHmm, let me look. If ther