xceeded` present in that order, it yields `pod_crash_no_stdout` and the backoff path runs — not `deadline_exceeded`. Kubernetes emits a single Failed condition so this is theoretical, and spec Failure Mode row 6 only requires \"either mapping is specific, never a silent merge\" (which the code delivers). Minor doc/requirement mismatch, not a functional bug.\n   Find/replace — in `<requirements>` step 3, replace:\n   > \"Do NOT merge the two conditions — a `DeadlineExceeded` condition always yields `deadline_exceeded`, even if a `BackoffLimitExceeded` condition is also present.\"\n   with:\n   > \"Do NOT merge the two conditions — a `DeadlineExceeded` condition yields `deadline_exceeded`, and a `BackoffLimitExceeded` condition yields the distinct pod-failure reason (whichever Failed condition iterates first wins; k8s emits a single Failed condition in practice).\"\n\n## Strengths\n- Every cited line number verified accurate to within a few lines — no stale references.\n- Requirements are fully load-bearing: exact function signatures, imports, and find-and-replace patterns for both the classifier and the existing tests.\n- Complete failure-mode and AC coverage, with each test scenario explicitly cross-referenced to the spec row it satisfies (req 10a \"satisfies ACs 2a, 3, and 4\").\n- Boundary-crossing handled correctly: the composed reason strings are asserted at the real `PublishFailure` boundary (level-2 integration through `HandleJob`), not just as constant-value tests.\n- The internal test file is properly justified (needs `w.(*jobWatcher).podLister`) and mirrors the repo's established internal/external precedent; the suite bootstrap (`TestPkg`/`RunSpecs`) will pick up the new specs.\n- Container-executable verification is honest (no exit-code-masked operator commands) and the prompt proactively bans `docker`/`kubectl`/`dark-factory` from itself.\n- YAGNI-clean: no scope creep beyond the spec's Goal, and non-goals (sweeper fallback, memory limit, permissions) are